    {
      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
      "Text": "The initial N set in the original operator code's date is in 2012 (the sim is for 2010-2011). The initial water in the sim has 2 settings that are contradictory: I used the 80% one. The pH in the original requires that the CERES pH factor is changed so adjusted the top pH to be 4.5.",
      "Name": "Memo",
      "ResourceName": null,
      "Children": [],
      "Enabled": true,
      "ReadOnly": false
    },
    {
      "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
      "ShowInDocs": false,
      "Name": "Validation",
      "ResourceName": null,
      "Children": [
        {
          "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
          "ShowInDocs": false,
          "Name": "HamiltonAU",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "### HP comments\nThe initial N set in the original operator code's date is in 2012 (the sim is for 2010-2011). The initial water in the sim has 2 settings that are contradictory: I used the 80% one. The pH in the original requires that the CERES pH factor is changed so adjusted the top pH to be 4.5.\n\n\n\n### Methods\nDuring 2010 and 2011, N2O emissions were monitored for\n1 year during wheat and fallow after conversion from long-term\nimproved pasture to cropland on adjacent plots (Mielenz et al.\n2016a). Soils had been under long-term pasture, consisting of\nsubterranean clover (Trifolium subterraneum L.) and perennial\nryegrass (Lolium perenne L.). Therefore, the soil was rich in\nstored organic carbon (Table 1). In each case, in the year before\nthe start of the experiment, a selective herbicide was used to\nreduce the pasture grasses and encourage a sward dominated by\nsubterranean clover. In late summer before cropping, the site\nwas completely sprayed out with glyphosate. Winter wheat\nvariety ‘Revenue’ was sown in April in each of the two years\nand harvested in January the following year. The effect of tillage\non N2O emissions was assessed by comparing zero tillage (ZT)\nby direct drilling the seed into sprayed out pasture with CT,\nwhere cultivation with several passes of discs, tynes and\nharrows preceded sowing (Table 1).",
              "Name": "Memo",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
